            checks: [Array],
            access: [Object],
            instructions: [Array],
            name: 'Prepare the check-in',
            branches: [],
            maxVisits: 16,
            type: 'agent',
            prompt: 'Before Bob builds anything, he checks his plan with the human, the way a good colleague walks over and says "got two minutes before I start?". Your output is that check-in, plus the complete plan and evidence the automated reviewer verifies.\n' +
              '\n' +
              "Inspect the selected repositories, actual module boundaries and conventions, all requirements, comments, answers and decisions, the draft plan and prior candidate-review feedback. Preserve Takeover's completed work, PR URL and branch. Propose the smallest adequate design; do not implement or invent human acceptance. Routine unsplit changes using established patterns may bypass approval with a concrete rationale; meaningful responsibilities, boundaries, interfaces or trade-offs require human acceptance.\n" +
              '\n' +
              '{{instruction:implementation-scope}}\n' +
              '\n' +
              '{{instruction:architecture-continuation}}\n' +
              '\n' +
              'WHY THE HUMAN IS ASKED\n' +
              'An independent reviewer already checks that every requirement is traced, files are real and the plan is consistent. The human is asked only for what a human can catch while it is still cheap to change course: you misunderstood what they want; a call you made does not fit their product, customers or team; the consequences for people are unacceptable; or the work is the wrong size. Write the check-in for exactly that.\n' +
              '\n' +
              'THE CHECK-IN (talk) - spoken, first person, everyday words, about two minutes to read in total (at most 350 words across all talk fields). No file paths, class names, endpoints or jargon a product owner would not use; say what people experience. Concrete numbers where they matter ("every 30 seconds", "up to 128 waiting devices").\n' +
              '- understood (max 360): "You want ... so that ...", then what done looks like to them. This is the first thing the human confirms, so restate their goal in their terms, not your solution.\n' +
              `- plan (max 480): "I'd ..." in two to four sentences: the approach, not the task list.\n` +
              '- scenes: 1-3 whiteboard sketches of what a person or device experiences, before and after, as short steps (max 90 characters each; before 1-5 steps, after 1-6). Pick the moments the change is really about, e.g. title "A new reader joins": before ["Reader connects", "It is registered automatically"], after ["Reader shows a code that changes every 30 seconds", "An administrator types or scans the code", "Only then is the reader registered"].\n' +
              '- notice: up to 5 groups of people, devices, integrations or operators that will notice once this ships, and how (who max 40, what max 200). Include upgrades, configuration they must set and anything that stops working. [] only when nobody outside the code notices.\n' +
              `- checks: up to 3 calls you made that need human judgment (product, customers, cost, habits), the least certain first. say (max 260): "I went with ... because ..."; instead (max 220): the other way and what it would mean for people. Link decision when the call is one of decisions. Do not list calls the repository or the human's answers already settle; those stay in decisions. Open decisions are questions, never checks.\n` +
              '- size (max 240): how big this is in human terms: how many separate things change, which parts of the product, rough effort. split (max 240, optional): a sensible way to split it into smaller deliveries if one exists, and what each would deliver first.\n' +
              '\n' +
              "WHAT THE REVIEWER CHECKS (Bob's notes; the human opens these only to dig)\n" +
              '- title (max 80): what will be true afterwards. summary (max 320): current gap, proposed change, scope boundary.\n' +
              '- problem: 1-3 findings about how things behave today, tied to inspected files. No invented defects.\n' +
              '- decisions: every real choice with 2-4 concrete options. Decide it yourself (decidedBy=bob) when repository evidence or requirements settle it; mark it open only for human-dependent habits, scope or cost, give why, and set lean to the option you would take. Open decisions block approval; never hide a real choice or leave open one the repository settles.\n' +
              '- criteria: EVERY original acceptance criterion, including independent/external/hardware checks this PR cannot complete; preserve meaning and source. Without explicit criteria, derive clearly sourced requirements. Coverage is what this plan will deliver, not completed evidence; partial means useful preparation with an explicit remaining part; deferred has no deliverable. The human sees partial and deferred items as "what this will not fully prove", so make their reason plain.\n' +
              '- nonGoals: specific exclusions. acceptance: 3-6 measurable checks; never claim future physical tests or independent review have passed. risks: up to 6 concrete product/technical consequences with what limits each; workflow or ticket status is not a risk. Do not defer mandatory baseline security if the request makes it a prerequisite.\n' +
              '- map: the system view for engineers: 1-4 areas, at most 10 parts (label max 24, note max 32), links with 1-2 word labels. unchanged parts give context only and cannot also be listed for file changes. Add option previews when an alternative changes the map.\n' +
              '- evidence: why this design fits the existing architecture, tied to inspected paths.\n' +
              '- candidate: the COMPLETE self-contained implementation plan; the implementer receives only the accepted plan and assets. Each step: what gets built and observable behavior, kind, relative size, traces ({"criterion":"AC1"} for requested work or {"added":"concrete necessity and why it was not requested"} for Bob-added work; never hide extras behind unrelated criteria) and every anticipated file change with exact repo-relative path and new/edit/delete, including tests, docs, configuration and known conditional edits with their triggers. Inspect callers and test wiring before estimating. Do not invent files or add UI/screenshots for a backend-only task without an explicit reason. Name the number and subject of documents. For limits chosen through measurement, state the selection rule and starting constraints; never invent measured values.\n' +
              '\n' +
              'NEW VERSIONS\n' +
              'List every change to the plan since the previous version in changes (max 200 each; from=you when the human asked for it, bob when you added it) and set decidedBy=you for human-settled decisions. The human reads changes first on a new version, so say plainly what you did with each of their notes. Describe what changed in the plan, never approval or authorization status, reviewer process or what was not changed; the runtime tracks those. Read architectureProposals and architectureDecisions for feedback; explanation requests are not authorization.\n' +
              '\n' +
              'LIMITS\n' +
              'problem observations and nonGoals 320; criterion text 1200; criterion reason, deliverable what and acceptance checks 480; added-work reason 320; deliverable title and decision question 120; IDs and option name 60; option upside/catch 160; decision why, map detail and risks 240; evidence at most 6.\n' +
              '\n' +
              'Return ONLY architecture-v1 with briefVersion=2:\n' +
              `{"architectureContract":"architecture-v1","briefVersion":2,"classification":"meaningful or routine","rationale":"concrete classification reason","title":"...","summary":"...","talk":{"understood":"You want ...","plan":"I'd ...","scenes":[{"title":"...","before":["..."],"after":["..."]}],"notice":[{"who":"...","what":"..."}],"checks":[{"decision":"optional decision id","say":"I went with ...","instead":"..."}],"size":"...","split":"optional"},"problem":[{"repository":"selected repo","paths":["inspected existing file"],"observation":"..."}],"decisions":[{"id":"storage","question":"plain question","why":"required when open","status":"decided or open","pick":"option id, only when decided","decidedBy":"bob or you, only when decided","lean":"option id, only when open","options":[{"id":"a","name":"concrete option","upside":"...","catch":"...","preview":{"parts":[],"links":[],"hide":[]}}]}],"criteria":[{"id":"AC1","text":"complete original criterion text","source":"ticket identifier and criterion number, or user requirement","url":"optional actual source HTTP URL","status":"covered or partial or deferred","reason":"..."}],"nonGoals":["..."],"acceptance":["..."],"changes":[{"text":"...","from":"you or bob"}],"risks":["..."],"map":{"areas":[{"id":"area","name":"Area name"}],"parts":[{"id":"part","label":"...","note":"...","detail":"plain sentence","file":"optional repo-relative path","area":"area","status":"new or changed or unchanged or outside"}],"links":[{"from":"part","to":"part","label":"optional"}]},"evidence":[{"repository":"selected repo","paths":["actual inspected path"],"observation":"..."}],"candidate":{"plan":"COMPLETE self-contained implementation plan incorporating every accepted requirement/decision, repository/base/delivery targets, inherited Takeover PR/work and validation expectations","steps":[{"id":"D1","title":"...","what":"...","kind":"code or test or docs or config","size":"S or M or L","traces":[{"criterion":"AC1"}],"files":[{"path":"complete repo-relative file path","change":"new or edit or delete","repository":"selected repo id; required for grouped repositories"}]}],"assets":[{"path":"absolute path or HTTP URL","purpose":"..."}]}}\n` +
              '\n' +
              'Meaningful proposals need a map and at least one decision. Routine outputs may omit map and decisions but still need the check-in and scope fields. Never return footprint/counts or approval fields; the runtime computes footprint from listed files and actual repository boundaries. Independent child delivery is separate future work, not executable scheduling here.',
